# 🧠 5. Important Concepts and Comparisons

| Concepts | Main Difference |
|---|---|
| Data Lifecycle vs. Data Engineering Lifecycle | Entire lifespan of data vs. engineering processes that prepare and deliver it |
| Source System vs. Data Platform | Where data originates vs. where it is stored, processed, or served |
| Push vs. Pull | Who initiates data transfer |
| Batch vs. Streaming | Processing groups of records vs. continuous events |
| Synchronous vs. Asynchronous | Waiting for an operation to complete vs. allowing more independent execution |
| Snapshot vs. CDC | Capturing dataset state vs. capturing changes |
| ETL vs. ELT | Transform before loading vs. transform after loading |
| Data Lake vs. Data Warehouse | Flexible storage for diverse data vs. analytical storage and querying |
| Schema-on-Write vs. Schema-on-Read | Apply structure during writing vs. interpret structure during reading |
| Monitoring vs. Observability | Track known metrics vs. understand system state from available evidence |
| Data Quality vs. Data Governance | Fitness of data for use vs. policies and accountability for managing data |
| Data Lineage vs. Metadata | Data origin and transformation history vs. descriptive information about data |
| DataOps vs. Orchestration | Broad operational practices vs. coordination of workflow execution |
| Idempotency vs. Exactly-Once Processing | Safe repetition of an operation vs. a stronger end-to-end processing guarantee |

